+## 9. The two single-dependency variants
+
+Both were requested after the consolidation landed. Neither replaces the
+consolidated tool; each is a strict, smaller build that removes a whole class of
+"which backend is this using?" questions.
+
+### 9.1 `g002/pdftool_fitz.py` — PyMuPDF only · 981 lines
+
+Same eleven subcommands (`text image view merge split extract shrink check outline
+info doctor`), same guard rails (`--max-size`, `--no-max-size`, `--max-pages`,
+`counts.skipped`, dry-run, `--json`, `--backup`), same JSON envelope. What is
+gone is the engine ladder:
+
+| in `pdftool.py` | in `pdftool_fitz.py` |
+|---|---|
+| 7 backends probed in a registry (`pymupdf → pypdf → pdfminer → poppler → OCR → …`) | `import pymupdf` (alias `fitz` on older builds) or nothing |
+| `doctor` lists what is installed and what each engine enables | `doctor` prints one line, exits 2 if PyMuPDF is absent |
+| `-e/--engine` selects a backend | no `--engine`; there is only one |
+| `shrink` shells out to Ghostscript / qpdf when present | `shrink -l 1\|2\|3` = `save(garbage=4, deflate=True)` → `+ rewrite_images(150→75 dpi, q75)` → `+ subset_fonts()` |
+
+Because there is no fallback, there is also no "degraded" path to test: either
+PyMuPDF is there and everything works, or the tool says `pip install pymupdf`
+and exits 2. `rewrite_images`/`subset_fonts` print to stdout, so they are wrapped
+in a suppressor — otherwise `--json` reports would be polluted.
+
+*Proven:* every subcommand against generated PDFs (3-page, 2-page, 100.1 MiB);
+`text`/`--per-page`/`-p`/`--stdout`, `image` png+jpg, `merge` (5 pages), `split`
+by page and `--max-mb`, `extract`, `check`, `shrink -l 1/2/3` (2,004 → 1,498
+bytes), `view` without a tty, `outline --summary/--html/--sync-titles`
+(`[[1,'One',1]]`), `--check-titles`, `--json`, page-limit skip, 100 MiB skip
+(`info` still reports it), bad page range → exit 2, and a simulated
+`pymupdf`-missing import → `doctor` exit 2.
+
+### 9.2 `rmc/rmc_ts.py` — `dh.TSStripper` only · 422 lines
+
+The simple version of `rmc/rmc.py`. It imports your module and trusts it:
+
+```python
+from dh import TSStripper
+stripped = TSStripper(...).strip(source)
+```
+
+No annotations, no libcst, no regex engine, no span maths, no AST fallback —
+`rmc.py` remains the one with three engines. What it keeps is the plumbing that
+makes an in-place rewriter safe, and the parallel loop:
+
+* **`ProcessPoolExecutor` and nothing else** — no `multiprocessing.Pool`, no
+  threads. One stripper per worker process, built once (`stripper_for()`) and
+  cached; `-j N` defaults to `min(8, cpu_count)`, and `-j 1` runs in-process so
+  a single file does not pay for a fork.
+* **Kwargs are filtered to what your `dh` actually declares** — `strip_comments`,
+  `strip_docstrings`, `remove_blank_lines`, `strip_all`, `preserve_shebangs`,
+  `preserve_module_docstring`, `preserve_directives`, `preserve_commented_code`,
+  `validate`. If the installed `TSStripper.__init__` has no `remove_blank_lines`,
+  the flag is reported as ignored instead of raising `TypeError`; if it takes
+  `**kwargs`, everything is passed through. This is why the file works against
+  older and newer revisions of `dh` alike.
+* **A compile gate on every write** — the stripped source must `ast.parse`, or
+  the file is left untouched and reported as a failure. A file that never parsed
+  (3.12-only syntax on a 3.11 interpreter) is reported as *skipped*, not failed.
+* Symlinks refused, `tokenize.detect_encoding` honoured, `--backup` writes
+  `FILE.bak` before the atomic replace, and the replace is `tmp → chmod →
+  os.replace` in the same directory.
+* `-c` / `-d` / `-a` / `--remove-blank-lines`, `-n/--dry-run`, `--check` (exit 1
+  iff work is pending), `--diff`, `--exclude GLOB`, `--no-recursive`, `-q`,
+  `--json`, `--version`.
+
+If `dh` is not importable the script prints the `ImportError` and exits 2 rather
+than silently doing something else; the import is at module scope but guarded, so
+`--help` still works.
+
+*Proven:* against a stand-in `dh` module in `/tmp` (real py-tree-sitter grammar,
+same `TSStripper(**kwargs).strip(source)` interface as `rmc_termux.py` uses):
+`-j 1` and `-j 8` produce byte-identical trees, output is idempotent, `--check`
+exits 1 on pending work and 0 after, `--diff`/`-n` write nothing, `--backup`
+keeps the original, `--remove-blank-lines` works and warns on old signatures,
+`node_modules/.venv` are skipped, `--json` parses, and 96 legacy files run in
+0.39 s at `-j 8` vs 0.71 s at `-j 1`. Three signature variants of `dh`
+(current, old 4-kwarg, `**kwargs`) were each driven end-to-end.
+
